body:
A Hazel program can now define a livelit and use it, live, below the definition:

```
let ^pct = {
  type Model = Int;
  type Action = Int;
  let init : Model = 50;
  let update = fun (m, a) : (Model, Action) -> a;
  let view = fun m : Model -> ...;   # HTML whose handlers emit Actions #
  let expand = fun m : Model -> m
} in
^pct(25) + ^pct(75)
```

Each use elaborates to `^pct.expand(model)` through the runtime binding, so shadowing and scoping are ordinary let semantics, and each use's model lives in its own argument syntax — dragging a slider rewrites the number in the text and the result recomputes. The definition form is a module (equivalently a labeled or positional tuple); type members are accepted but not yet load-bearing; helpers and an optional `size = (w, h)` are ordinary members. Only `expand` runs during statics, built syntactically — no evaluation during elaboration. `update`/`view` run at render time via HazelDOM/MvuShape from the MVU work, so definitions must be closed.

Two doc slides ship as demos ("Livelits / Define a Slider", "Livelits / Color Picker" — an hwb picker with a clickable gradient square, which motivated a new `OnClickAt` attribute delivering element-relative click coordinates). Also fixes a pre-existing bug where every livelit projector in text-loaded slides rendered "No livelit found" (`get_model` didn't look through Projector/Parens wrappers).

17 new tests (`UserLivelits`); suite at 3080 + doc-slide reparse green. Follow-up thinking on probe feedback inside definitions is recorded in `plans/livelit-probe-feedback.md`.

